To implement JSP pages for displaying user data in a web application, you can follow a structure 
where the user information is passed from a Servlet to the JSP page, which then dynamically renders
it in HTML format. 
Below is a step-by-step example 
demonstrating how to create a simple system to 
display user data using JSP and Servlets.

1. Create the User Model Class
The User class will represent a user, containing properties such as id, name, email, and age.

package com.example;

public class User {
    private int id;
    private String name;
    private String email;
    private int age;

    // Constructor
    public User(int id, String name, String email, int age) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.email = email;
        this.age = age;
    }

    // Getters and Setters
    public int getId() { return id; }
    public void setId(int id) { this.id = id; }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public String getEmail() { return email; }
    public void setEmail(String email) { this.email = email; }

    public int getAge() { return age; }
    public void setAge(int age) { this.age = age; }
}

2. Create the UserManager Class
This class will simulate fetching user data from a database or service.

package com.example;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class UserManager {
    
    // Method to get a list of users
    public List<User> getAllUsers() {
        List<User> users = new ArrayList<>();
        
        // Creating dummy users for demonstration
        users.add(new User(1, "John Doe", "john.doe@example.com", 30));
        users.add(new User(2, "Jane Smith", "jane.smith@example.com", 25));
        users.add(new User(3, "Alice Johnson", "alice.johnson@example.com", 28));
        
        return users;
    }
}

3. Create the UserServlet Class
The UserServlet will handle requests from the client (browser), interact with UserManager, and forward the data to the JSP page for rendering.
package com.example;

import java.io.*;
import javax.servlet.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;
import java.util.List;

public class UserServlet extends HttpServlet {
    
    private UserManager userManager;

    public void init() {
        userManager = new UserManager(); // Initialize the user manager
    }

    // Handle GET requests to display user data
    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws ServletException, IOException {
        List<User> users = userManager.getAllUsers(); // Fetch all users
        request.setAttribute("users", users); // Set users as a request attribute

        // Forward to the JSP page to display users
        RequestDispatcher dispatcher = request.getRequestDispatcher("/userList.jsp");
        dispatcher.forward(request, response);
    }
}


4. Create the userList.jsp Page
This JSP page will receive the user data passed by the UserServlet and render it in an HTML table.

<%@ page contentType="text/html;charset=UTF-8" language="java" %>
<html>
<head>
    <title>User List</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>List of Users</h1>

    <table border="1">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>ID</th>
                <th>Name</th>
                <th>Email</th>
                <th>Age</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <c:forEach var="user" items="${users}">
                <tr>
                    <td>${user.id}</td>
                    <td>${user.name}</td>
                    <td>${user.email}</td>
                    <td>${user.age}</td>
                </tr>
            </c:forEach>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</body>
</html>



